    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the email-uniqueness rule so creating an owner whose lower-cased email already belongs to another owner returns **409 Conflict**, following the existing `existsByTelephone` pattern across every layer:\n\n- **`OwnerRepository`** \u2014 added `existsByEmailIgnoreCase(String email)`, mirroring the telephone method's contract/docs.\n- **`SpringDataOwnerRepository`** \u2014 declared the derived query method (Spring Data resolves `existsByEmailIgnoreCase` automatically).\n- **`JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl`** \u2014 JPQL `COUNT` with `LOWER(owner.email) = LOWER(:email)`.\n- **`JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`** \u2014 SQL `COUNT(*)` with `LOWER(email) = LOWER(:email)`.\n- **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `existsOwnerByEmail(...)` delegating to the repository, marked `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` like its telephone sibling.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a conflict check next to the other uniqueness checks, guarded so it only runs when an email was supplied (email is optional). The `Owner` model already canonicalizes email to lower case on `setEmail`, and the repository query lowers on both sides, so the comparison is genuinely case-insensitive.\n\nEach piece lives in its natural layer (persistence query in the repositories, business check in the service, HTTP status mapping in the controller) and reuses the established telephone-uniqueness structure rather than duplicating logic. `mvnw test` \u2192 BUILD SUCCESS, `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` passes.",
